Thank you — that’s a nice icon. If it’s alright with the team, I’ll suggest two gentle options (one minimal, one styled) so we can pick what keeps the UI consistent and everyone comfortable.

Option 1 — minimal and very safe
- Add a <title> element inside the SVG. It’s the simplest, is supported by assistive tech and browsers, and won’t change layout or styling. If the design lead prefers minimal visual change, this is often preferred.

Example:
<svg ...>
  <title>Alert icon — more info</title>
  <!-- rest of your SVG -->
</svg>

Option 2 — styled, accessible tooltip (recommended when we want custom appearance)
- Keep the SVG inline in the HTML and position an HTML tooltip element over it. This gives full styling control and can be made keyboard-accessible and screen-reader friendly. Below is a compact, accessible approach you might like; if this layout needs to match a component library or design tokens, I can adapt it.

HTML + CSS + JS example (paste into a page):
